  "query_preview": "..The rain from the departing system that has now\nmoved off into Texas has moistened the lower boundary layer\nsubstantially leading to some areas of patchy fog and low\nceilings. This should dry up some late this morning into this \nafternoon as a fast moving ridge brings some brief drier air and \nclear skies, with temperatures warming up to mid November \naverages. Some patchy fog may be possible again tonight into early\nMonday morning but not as widespread as this morning. \n\nMost of Monday will be dry with seasonal temperatures before the\nnext West Coast storm system moves into the area. This system will\nlast all day Tuesday and depart the region Wednesday morning.\nCurrent QPF amounts look pretty close to the last storm,\nespecially over the western zones, and a bit drier in the eastern\nzones. With a more northerly origin from this storm, snow levels\nwill be lower, dropping down to 6500-7000ft before it gets out of\nhere Wednesday morning. As the snow level drop occurs later in the\nstorm, accumulations will be light with only the Kaibab Plateau,\nSan Francisco Peaks and highest parts of the White Mountains\nseeing anything over an inch. A brief dusting may occur as low as\n7000ft but shouldn't be enough to cause travel problems.  \n\nWednesday will see another break with temperatures recovering to\nseasonal averages before the next trough moves in Thursday\nafternoon and lasts into late Friday night. This could be a \ncolder storm system with lower snow levels, possibly down to \n6000ft. At this time it looks like its trending a little drier \nthan the midweek one so if that holds true snow accumulations will\nbe limited to a dusting. Will continue to monitor and see if it \ncontinues to trend drier or if reverses course into something of \nmore concern.",
